---
// Terms and Conditions (SITE-STRUCTURE.md page 17). The text is strale-frontend's
// src/pages/Terms.tsx, word for word: it binds Moonlighter AB, so any change to it is Petter's.
// Only the look and three link targets changed: the catalogue link points at /tools (the text
// still says strale.dev/capabilities, which redirects there at the switch-over), and links
// use this site's paths.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LegalDoc from "../components/LegalDoc.astro";

const LAST_UPDATED = "29 April 2026";
const CONTACT_EMAIL = "compliance@strale.io";
---
<Base title="Terms and Conditions" description="Terms governing use of the Strale API and platform. Operated by Moonlighter AB.">
  <LegalDoc title="Terms and Conditions" updated={LAST_UPDATED}>
    <p slot="intro">
      These terms govern your use of the Strale platform and API
      (the &ldquo;Service&rdquo;). By signing up for an account or
      using the Service, you agree to these terms. Questions:{" "}
      <a class="link" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <section id="parties">
      <h2>1. The parties</h2>
      <p>
        The Service is operated by <strong>Moonlighter AB</strong> (Sweden,
        organisationsnummer 559395-7979) (&ldquo;we&rdquo;, &ldquo;us&rdquo;).
        &ldquo;You&rdquo; means the person or organisation using the Service.
        If you are accepting these terms on behalf of an organisation, you
        confirm that you have authority to bind that organisation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="service">
      <h2>2. The Service</h2>
      <p>
        The Service is an API platform that lets you call data and validation
        capabilities, including the Payee Assurance product. Capabilities
        fetch data from public registries, licensed data providers, and our
        own algorithmic checks, and return structured responses with
        provenance and an audit record.
      </p>
      <p>
        Capability behaviour, pricing, and coverage are described on the
        public catalog at{" "}
        <a href="/tools">strale.dev/capabilities</a>{" "}
        and are part of these terms by reference. We may add, change, or
        remove capabilities; material changes are communicated under{" "}
        <a href="#changes">Section 12</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="account">
      <h2>3. Accounts and API keys</h2>
      <p>
        To use authenticated parts of the Service you create an account and
        generate API keys. You are responsible for keeping your API keys
        confidential and for activity performed using them. Notify us at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>{" "}
        if you suspect a key has been compromised so we can revoke it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some capabilities are available without an account via x402
        pay-per-use; in that mode, payment of the on-chain invoice is
        your authentication and you accept these terms by paying.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="billing">
      <h2>4. Pricing, billing, and refunds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Wallet model.</strong> You top up a prepaid balance via
          Stripe and the Service deducts per-call fees as you make API calls.
          Top-ups are non-refundable except as set out below or as required
          by applicable consumer-protection law.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>x402 pay-per-use.</strong> Each call is paid on-chain in
          USDC at the moment it is executed. There are no recurring charges
          and no subscription.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Free trial.</strong> New accounts receive trial credits as
          announced on{" "}
          <a href="/pricing">strale.dev/pricing</a>.
          Trial credits have no cash value and cannot be redeemed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Failed calls are not charged.</strong> If a capability
          execution fails on our side or returns a structured error before
          the upstream call succeeds, no fee is debited.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account closure refund.</strong> If you close your account,
          unused wallet balance is refundable on request within 60 days of
          closure. After 60 days the unused balance is forfeit.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="acceptable-use">
      <h2>5. Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>You agree not to use the Service to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>break the law, evade sanctions, or facilitate fraud;</li>
        <li>process personal data of EU/UK data subjects without an appropriate lawful basis;</li>
        <li>circumvent rate limits, quotas, or the Service&rsquo;s authentication;</li>
        <li>resell the Service as your own or build a competing product whose primary purpose is to mirror Strale&rsquo;s capability catalog;</li>
        <li>scrape or systematically extract Strale&rsquo;s own metadata (capability descriptions, test results, pricing) outside of the documented public endpoints;</li>
        <li>use AI-assisted capability outputs as the sole basis for decisions with legal or significant effects on a person without human review.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For capabilities that involve special-category data under Art. 9 GDPR
        (e.g. PEP and sanctions screening) or criminal-conviction data under
        Art. 10 GDPR (e.g. adverse-media checks), you confirm that you have
        identified and rely on an appropriate condition under Art. 9(2) /
        Art. 10 GDPR (or the UK equivalent) for processing such data.
      </p>
      <p>
        We may suspend or terminate accounts that breach this section.
        Serious or repeated breaches result in termination without refund.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="data">
      <h2>6. Your data and ours</h2>
      <p>
        You retain all rights to the data you send to the Service and to the
        structured responses you receive. We process your data only to
        provide the Service and as set out in our{" "}
        <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
        We do not use your data to train models and we do not sell it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Strale retains all rights to the platform itself: the executors,
        orchestration, audit-trail engine, test infrastructure, and
        accompanying documentation. You receive a non-exclusive,
        non-transferable licence to use the Service during the term of these
        terms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data fetched from public registries and similar third-party sources
        is governed by those sources&rsquo; own terms. We pass through that
        data to you with the provenance attached so you can verify its
        origin.
      </p>
      <p>
        For capabilities that wrap third-party screening providers
        (currently: Dilisense for sanctions, PEP, and adverse-media checks;
        Serper for adverse-media fallback), the upstream provider&rsquo;s
        own service terms govern our access to their data. Their liability
        to us is contractually limited; those limitations may constrain our
        practical recourse, and therefore yours, in the event of upstream
        data error, omission, or outage. Provenance fields on each response
        identify the upstream provider so you can apply your own
        due-diligence judgement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="availability">
      <h2>7. Service availability</h2>
      <p>
        We aim to keep the Service available continuously, but we do not
        offer a contractual uptime SLA at this stage. We may schedule
        maintenance windows, throttle abusive traffic, and depend on
        third-party data sources whose own availability we do not control.
        When an upstream source is unavailable, we serve cached results where
        we have them and return a structured error otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        We publish quality and reliability scores for each capability via
        our public quality endpoint; those scores are our best estimate of
        execution quality and not a contractual warranty.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="warranty">
      <h2>8. Warranty and liability</h2>
      <p>
        The Service is provided <strong>&ldquo;as is&rdquo;</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>&ldquo;as available&rdquo;</strong>. To the maximum extent
        permitted by law, we disclaim all warranties not expressly granted in
        these terms, including warranties of merchantability, fitness for a
        particular purpose, and non-infringement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Capability outputs are evidence to inform your decisions, not
        decisions in themselves. You are responsible for any action you take
        based on a Service response. Where a capability returns an explicit{" "}
        <code>decision-readiness</code>{" "}
        object, you should consult that object before treating a response as
        actionable.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compliance and screening capabilities specifically.</strong>{" "}
        Capabilities marketed for compliance use &mdash; including sanctions
        screening (<code>sanctions-check</code>),
        politically-exposed-persons screening (<code>pep-check</code>),
        and adverse-media screening (<code>adverse-media-check</code>)
        &mdash; return evidence about the matches found in upstream data
        sources at the moment of the call. They are not regulatory
        determinations. A &ldquo;no match&rdquo; or{" "}
        <code>is_sanctioned: false</code>{" "}
        response does not mean the subject is free of sanctions, PEP status,
        or adverse coverage; it means the upstream provider&rsquo;s index,
        as configured at that moment, did not surface a match for the query
        as written. You remain responsible for: (a) querying with
        appropriate name variants, (b) applying your organisation&rsquo;s
        risk appetite and additional controls, and (c) any regulatory
        determination you are required to make. Where applicable law
        requires human review of an automated decision (e.g. EU AML
        Directive, UK MLR 2017 Regulation 28A, GDPR Art. 22), that review
        is your obligation, not ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        To the maximum extent permitted by law, our aggregate liability
        arising out of or related to these terms in any 12-month period is
        limited to the greater of (a) the fees you paid us in that period or
        (b) <strong>EUR 1,000</strong>. We are not liable for indirect,
        incidental, special, or consequential damages, or for loss of
        profits, revenue, data, or goodwill.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in these terms limits liability for fraud, gross negligence,
        wilful misconduct, or any liability that cannot be limited under
        applicable law.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="indemnification">
      <h2>9. Indemnification</h2>
      <p>
        You agree to indemnify and hold us harmless from third-party claims
        arising out of (a) your use of the Service in breach of these terms,
        (b) your use of capability outputs in a way that infringes the rights
        of a data subject or third party, or (c) data you submit that you do
        not have the right to process.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="termination">
      <h2>10. Term and termination</h2>
      <p>
        These terms apply for as long as you have an account or are using
        the Service. You may close your account at any time from your
        dashboard or by emailing us. We may suspend or terminate your
        account on notice for material breach of these terms; in cases of
        unlawful use, security risk, or sanctions risk, we may suspend
        immediately and notify you afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sections that by their nature should survive termination (data
        rights, warranty disclaimers, liability limits, indemnification,
        governing law) survive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changes">
      <h2>11. Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms. Material changes (pricing, liability,
        governing law, acceptable use) are communicated by email at least
        30 days before they take effect. Non-material changes take effect on
        posting. Continued use of the Service after changes take effect
        means you accept the updated terms.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="law">
      <h2>12. Governing law and disputes</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the laws of Sweden, excluding its
        conflict-of-law rules and the UN Convention on Contracts for the
        International Sale of Goods.
      </p>
      <p>
        Disputes will be resolved in the Stockholm District Court (Stockholms
        tingsrätt) as the court of first instance, except that we may bring
        claims relating to unauthorised access, fee non-payment, or
        intellectual-property infringement in any court of competent
        jurisdiction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mandatory consumer-protection rights of consumers resident in the
        EU/UK are not affected by this clause.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="general">
      <h2>13. General</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>If any provision is held unenforceable, the rest remain in effect.</li>
        <li>You may not assign these terms without our consent; we may assign them as part of a corporate transaction.</li>
        <li>These terms (with the Privacy Policy) are the entire agreement between us on the subject.</li>
        <li>Failure to enforce a provision is not a waiver.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </LegalDoc>
</Base>
